  "notes": {
    "vision": "The yard of the trailer is a place you want to spend a summer in: warm, slightly worn, never busy. Every object is a little simpler and softer than the real one, but built the way the real one is built, so it reads instantly and still feels true. When two goals fight, the game camera decides: if it does not read from where the player stands, it is wrong however accurate it is.",
    "value": "Most of every picture lives in the middle values, with a light sky and light walls on top and only small dark accents (iron, rubber, tyre, deep shadow under eaves). Keep big shapes in one value group each; a model that breaks into many small light-dark patches reads as noise from the game camera.",
    "colour": "Colours are soft and warm, a little greyed, with the haze of a summer afternoon. Saturation is spent on a few accents only: the red mower, the green and yellow nozzle. Base colours are painted a touch darker and more saturated than they look on screen, because the lavender sky light and the haze lift and soften them.",
    "hue": "The yard is green and yellow-green, the buildings grey-blue and white, the accents red. New colours join one of these families; a colour from outside them (a neon, a cold cyan) needs a reason.",
    "light": "Light is warm and shadows are cool and coloured, never neutral black: the lavender sky fills every shadow. Nothing is painted into a texture that the light should do: no baked highlights, no painted shadows.",
    "detail": "Large calm surfaces with detail where it explains the object: board lines, a hinge, a tread block. Below the detail floor a feature is painted or left out. The spectrum of the sources is calmer than a photograph (a steeper Fourier slope), so a model with fine noise everywhere is off-style even if each piece is right.",
    "shape": "Simple, slightly rounded, readable forms; one solid piece for one real piece. A silhouette at 32 px must still say what the object is.",
    "materials": "The finish comes from what the thing is made of: matte for wood, rubber and rough plastic, satin for paint and powder coat, glossy only for enamel and glass. A real grain lies under the paint; every board and shingle is a touch different in tone.",
    "classes": "Each class has its own detail floor and budget: what a hand holds in first person is seen up close and gets its small parts; a building is seen from metres away and gets its boards, not its nails.",
    "do_dont": "Each pair shows the style beside a near miss of the same model: the mistakes a generator makes easily. Every near miss names the rule it breaks and the check that catches it; the numbers below each one say what the measured checks see, and where only the review can see it, the page says so.",
    "in_game": "The trailer's frame beside the same view in the game: the final test of the style is here, under the game's own light and shader."
  },
